# [Vault Name]

> **Startup.** Read in this order at the start of any substantive session (skip for trivial or purely conversational turns):
>
> 1. **This file (`CLAUDE.md`).** Routing.
> 2. **`Daily/<today>.md`.** If today's file is absent, read the most recently modified file in `Daily/` instead.
> 3. **`Tasks/todo.md`.** What is open right now.
> 4. **`Tasks/lessons.md`.** Rules earned the hard way. Scan for anything relevant to the current ask rather than reading the whole file once it grows.
> 5. **`Rules-of-Engagement.md`.** Active operational rules, led by the non-negotiables.
>
> Ground the clock (`date`) before the first response of a session if timing matters to the task.

This is [Owner Name]'s primary context vault: an Obsidian knowledge base and the operating memory for every AI session touching this work. Durable state lives here as markdown so it survives across sessions.

Two of the files here are for the owner rather than for you. `Start-here.md` explains the vault in plain language, and `Home.md` is the landing page Obsidian opens on. Keep both readable by a person: no routing tables, no rule numbering, no vault jargon. If a change would make either one read like this file, put it in this file instead.

Three rules override everything else:

1. **Discoverability over cleverness.** Every folder has a `CLAUDE.md` routing index that tells the next session what lives there and where new information should land. The discovery chain from this file to any leaf note is at most three reads.
2. **No orphans among curated notes.** Every authored note links to at least one other note, and every folder is reachable from this file. This does NOT apply to dated capture leaves (`Daily/`, `_inbox/`) or to skill machinery.
3. **One home per fact.** If a piece of information has two plausible homes, pick one and link from the other. Never duplicate authoritative content.

